<h2 id="about">What this tool does</h2>
<p>Your body burns calories just to stay alive — breathing, circulating blood, keeping your brain running — and that baseline is the Basal Metabolic Rate, or BMR. This calculator derives BMR with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, the formula most dietitians still prefer because it beats older ones on average adults. It needs four honest inputs: sex, age, height and weight. Change any one and the answer recomputes immediately.</p>
<p>BMR alone is not what you eat, because you also move. Multiplying BMR by an activity factor gives Total Daily Energy Expenditure, or TDEE — the calories that keep your weight exactly where it is. Five tiers are offered, from a desk-bound 1.2 up to an athlete's 1.9. Pick the one that describes a normal week, not your single most active day, or the number runs high.</p>
<p>Finally you choose a goal: lose, maintain or gain. Weight loss and gain use the well-known roughly-7,700-calories-per-kilo logic, applied here as a safe percentage of TDEE rather than an extreme fixed deficit. The result block shows BMR, TDEE, your target calories and a beginner-friendly protein-carb-fat split in grams, so you leave with numbers you can actually plan meals around.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use the Calorie Calculator <span style="color:var(--cy)">| Step by Step Guide</span></h2>
<div class="howto"><ol><li><span>Select sex and enter age, height in centimetres and weight in kilograms.</span></li><li><span>Choose the activity level that matches a typical week, from sedentary to very active.</span></li><li><span>Set a goal — lose, maintain or gain weight.</span></li><li><span>Press Calculate to see BMR, TDEE, target calories and the macro gram split.</span></li><li><span>Revisit the activity level every few weeks as your routine changes.</span></li></ol></div>
<span class="ru" data-ru lang="ros">Apna sex, umar, qad aur wazan likhein, phir activity level aur goal (lose/maintain/gain) chunein. 'Calculate' dabane par BMR, TDEE, target calories aur protein-carb-fat ka gram split mil jayega. Sab hisaab aapke browser me hota hai.</span>
<h2 id="features">Features</h2>
<ul class="feat-list"><li>Mifflin-St Jeor BMR, the standard clinical equation</li><li>Five-tier activity multiplier to reach realistic TDEE</li><li>Goal adjustment for fat loss, maintenance or lean gain</li><li>Protein / carbohydrate / fat grams shown for your target</li><li>Live recompute — edit an input and results refresh</li><li>No login and no stored history; numbers stay on your device</li></ul>
<h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
<ul><li>Weigh yourself weekly and update the weight field — a stale input is the usual reason targets feel wrong.</li><li>Choose the honest activity tier: if you train hard but sit all day, 'moderate' is usually right, not 'active'.</li><li>Losing more than about 1% of body weight a week is aggressive; the deficit here is deliberately gentle.</li><li>Treat the macro split as a starting template, then shift protein up if you are resistance-training.</li><li>Very low calorie targets are a signal the inputs are off — recalculate before committing to a plan.</li></ul>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>Which formula does this use?</summary><p>Mifflin-St Jeor: for men BMR = 10×kg + 6.25×cm − 5×age + 5, and for women the same with −161 instead of +5. It is the current first-line choice over the older Harris-Benedict equation.</p></details>
<details><summary>What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?</summary><p>BMR is the energy your body uses at complete rest. TDEE multiplies BMR by your activity level and adds movement, so it is the true maintenance calorie figure.</p></details>
<details><summary>How accurate is the macro split?</summary><p>It is a simple default ratio (roughly 30% protein, 40% carbs, 30% fat) meant to give a starting point, not a medically prescribed diet. Adjust ratios to your preferences and goals.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is the calorie calculator free to use?</summary><p>Yes — Calorie Calculator has no usage cap, no sign-up and no watermark, and it is not a trial: the page is a static file on https://wafasmart.tech with no billing backend behind it. </p></details>
<details><summary>Does it account for muscle mass?</summary><p>No formula that uses only height, weight, age and sex can distinguish muscle from fat. Very muscular people should read the BMR as an under-estimate.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does anything get uploaded when I use calorie calculator?</summary><p>No. Calorie Calculator reads your body measurements straight into page memory, works on it locally and hands the result back as a download — there is no upload request to intercept, store or leak, and no log of what you processed.</p></details>
